  "result": "**Gate: `OK`** on the first run (1 of 6 used) — 3 witnesses, 2 names, reads 2 atoms, 1 narrowing.\n\n**Body** (`library/TrafficSpec/Concepts/position/direction_component.lean`): the scalar projection of the road user's velocity on the region's designated direction,\n\n```\nvx·dx + vy·dy      vx,vy = kin_velocity u (\"x\",\"y\")      dx,dy = st_lane_direction r (\"x\",\"y\")\n```\n\nBoth vector atoms are read through their component literals (Core's `evalMeasure` takes `[entity, component]`). Only the formals `u` and `r` appear — no bound variable, no region quantifier — so the quantity stays geometric, and the unknown clause falls out of the arithmetic: a product is unknown where either factor is, so the sum is unknown exactly where velocity is unobserved or the region carries no designated direction.\n\n**Narrowing declared**: the projection written as a two-component world-frame sum, on the span naming the component; the contract names a component along a direction without saying how it is formed, and the atoms expose components only.\n\n**Witnesses** (`library/TrafficSpecWitness/…`): region 100 a one-way roadway (−60,−60)–(60,60) m carrying direction `(3, 4)` m — off-axis so both components do work, and exact under the atom's normalisation (it reads as exactly the unit vector (0.6, 0.8)). A car at 8 m/s over three observations: with the designation the projection is exactly +8 m/s (`holds`), against it exactly −8 m/s (`fails`), and with `st.lane_direction` masked it is unknown (`blind`). Instance bounds [+4, +12] m/s are the caller's figures.\n\n`task_result.md` is written in the working directory. Nothing in the record turned out to be wrong, so no dependency defect and no further runs.",
